    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, \"material accretion\" sounds a lot more professional than \"a pile of junk,\" i guess lol. but it's hard to stay methodical when it feels so... constant. like, i'll clear out one corner of a box, and by the time i've moved to the next folder, i swear i see more of those little bits of string or paper just peeking out from under the edges of the documents. it almost feels like they're actively avoiding being picked up. \n\nis there any specific way to handle them so they don't just get mixed back in with the important stuff? i'm worried if i don't deal with them properly, they're just going to keep popping up and making a mess of the whole cataloging process.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you're fighting a losing battle against the sheer *volume* of tiny debris, and that feeling of things actively hiding is very relatable! To manage this, you need a dedicated \"quarantine\" process for the detritus. Don't just toss them; treat every scrap as a potential piece of history, even if it's just a bit of string.\n\nWhen you find one of these tiny pieces, immediately place it into a small, labeled envelope or designated tray—a \"Debris Holding Unit,\" if you will. This separates the unwanted material from the primary documents, preventing it from re-contaminating your sorted files. You can then process this debris later, perhaps by running it through a sieve or examining it under magnification to see if it has any context. This keeps your main cataloging flow clean and focused.",
      "source": "target",
